The engine itself (api, engine, runtime, frontends, testkit) is original work.

The corpus module (balticporter/corpus) additionally ships hand-written Scala support files that a
port's policy injects into generated code. Each of those files states its origin and licence in
its header. They are ports of, or derived from:

  libGDX, including its GWT backend emulation   Apache-2.0   https://github.com/libgdx/libgdx
  gdx-ai                                        Apache-2.0   https://github.com/libgdx/gdx-ai
  Ashley                                        Apache-2.0   https://github.com/libgdx/ashley
  gdx-gltf                                      Apache-2.0   https://github.com/mgsx-dev/gdx-gltf
  SGE (Scala Game Engine)                       Apache-2.0   https://github.com/kubuszok/sge
  flexmark-java                                 BSD-2-Clause https://github.com/vsch/flexmark-java
  Liqp                                          MIT          https://github.com/bkiers/Liqp

The repository (not the published artifacts) also holds test fixtures under
balticporter/frontend-ts/src/test/resources: syntax-tree exports of mermaid (MIT), KaTeX (MIT),
terser (BSD-2-Clause), dart-sass (MIT) and Rough.js and its companion libraries (MIT).

Code generated by Baltic Porter is a derivative work of the Java sources it was generated from and
carries their licence, not this one: every generated file opens with a header naming the upstream
project, the upstream commit and the original licence, and the upstream notice files a port declares
are copied next to the generated sources.
